  {
   "type": "faq",
   "h": "Questions validation and engineering teams ask",
   "items": [
    {
     "q": "You are a new supplier. How do we know the skid will pass IQ/OQ on schedule?",
     "a": "You do not have to take it on trust. Your quality unit approves the FAT protocol before we start. You witness a wet FAT in which every sequence, alarm, interlock and divert runs on water, and you review the executed report and the turnover package before the skid ships. Deviations found at FAT are closed in our shop, not on your site. Ask for the sample turnover package to check the format before you order."
    },
    {
     "q": "Can our engineering and QA teams witness the FAT?",
     "a": "Yes: in person at [test bay location], remotely over [remote witness platform], or both. The FAT and SAT page lists what a remote witness can and cannot see."
    },
    {
     "q": "Are the skids registered for pressure and the panels certified?",
     "a": "Pressure-retaining parts are registered under CSA B51 with a Canadian registration number (CRN) for your province [confirm who registers and for which provinces]. Control panels are built and marked to [panel standard and certification mark held]."
    },
    {
     "q": "How do you document material traceability and weld quality?",
     "a": "Every product-contact component is traced to its EN 10204 3.1 certificate by heat number. Every weld has a line in the weld log, tied to the weld map, with [borescope inspection percentage] borescope inspection recorded. The Fabrication page shows each field and what it proves."
    },
    {
     "q": "What does FAT prove if the skid runs on your test water, not our feed?",
     "a": "FAT proves function: every sequence, alarm, interlock and divert, a sanitization cycle reaching temperature at every probe, instrument loop checks and outlet quality on our test water. Performance on your feed water is proven at SAT and OQ. We write the protocols so your quality unit can leverage FAT results in qualification, in line with ASTM E2500 and ISPE commissioning and qualification guidance, where it accepts that approach."
    },
    {
     "q": "How do you handle cold feed water in winter?",
     "a": "RO output falls as water cools. A membrane array rated at 25 °C can lose a third or more of its output at 5 °C, and many Canadian supplies reach that in winter. We size the array at your lowest recorded feed temperature, or add a heat exchanger that holds a design temperature, and we show the capacity at both ends of your range in the mass balance."
    },
    {
     "q": "Why EDI instead of mixed-bed ion exchange?",
     "a": "EDI regenerates continuously in an electric field. There are no acid or caustic deliveries, no regeneration downtime, no resin exhaustion events to investigate and no resin beds for bacteria to colonise between exchanges."
    },
    {
     "q": "Single or double pass RO?",
     "a": "Your feed water decides. High alkalinity, dissolved solids or silica, or a WFI still or Pure Steam generator downstream, point to double pass. Soft, low-alkalinity feed that makes PW only usually suits a single pass ahead of EDI. We show both options against your analysis when the choice is close."
    },
    {
     "q": "Our municipal supply is chloraminated. What changes?",
     "a": "Chloramine must be removed before the RO, because polyamide membranes do not tolerate it. Breaking it releases ammonia. RO rejects ammonium well, but if we raise pH to control carbon dioxide, more of it becomes dissolved ammonia gas, which passes the membrane and reaches the EDI. So we design the chloramine removal and the CO₂ strategy together. Pretreatment covers the removal step."
    },
    {
     "q": "Hot water or chemical sanitization?",
     "a": "Hot water at 80 °C or more leaves no residual to rinse out and test for, and the cycle is proven by temperature at the coldest point. Chemical sanitization suits membranes that are not rated for heat, but adds a residual test to every cycle. Sanitization frequency is set from your trend data during PQ, not fixed at design."
    },
    {
     "q": "Can the same skid feed a WFI still or a Pure Steam generator?",
     "a": "Yes. We size it for your PW draw plus the still or generator feed demand, and design to that equipment's feed limits, typically on conductivity, silica, chloride and total hardness. Those limits go into the functional specification so they are tested at FAT."
    },
    {
     "q": "Our feed comes from a private well. Is that a problem?",
     "a": "Not in itself. USP and Ph. Eur. require PW to be made from water of drinking water quality, so you need to show your well meets it. Iron, manganese, hardness and silica then drive the pretreatment, and we ask for analyses from more than one season because well chemistry moves."
    },
    {
